---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import TitleLine from '../../../../components/editorial/TitleLine.astro';
import ArticleBody from '../../../../components/editorial/ArticleBody.astro';
import WorkFigure from '../../../../components/application-caching/bounce/BounceFigure.astro';
import '../../../../styles/explorables.css';
import '../../../../styles/application-caching.css';
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return process.env.APPLICATION_CACHING_DRAFT_PREVIEW === '1' ? [{params:{view:undefined}}] : [];
}
---
<Base title="An answer, ready" description="A new animation study for application caching." readingPath="/">
  <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
  <article class="visual-essay cache-article" data-pagefind-ignore="all">
    <header class="essay-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Application caching · New visual study</p>
      <h1>An answer, ready.</h1>
      <TitleLine type="Introduction" />
    </header>
    <ArticleBody>
      <p>The first request joins two tables. The next requests find the answer already in Redis.</p>
      <WorkFigure />
      <p>The server checks Redis first. On a miss, it asks the database for the joined result and saves a copy. While that copy is usable, later requests can skip the join. Redis is one of many cache implementations.</p>
      <section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources">
        <h2 id="sources">Sources and model notes</h2>
        <p>This illustration uses <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/cache-aside">cache-aside</a>. The invented product is north’s P7 blue mug, in English and GBP. Layout and animation timing are explanatory, not measurements. Products supplies the name; Prices supplies the amount. The join matches product and shop, with English and GBP selected. All requests happen within one cache lifetime, with no product update.</p>
      </section>
    </ArticleBody>
  </article>
</Base>
